{"cluster_id": "C-3038", "topic": "parentFoldStates contains only rows that existed", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B049-008"], "canonical_claim": "`parentFoldStates` contains only rows that existed in the adopted parent seal and is used for baseline and cursor validation, preserving the distinction between no cursor and a default cursor state.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": [".superpowers/sdd/2026-07-28-cas-ref-chain-stage-b-catalog/task-5-checkpoint75b-round-plan-report.md#implemented-boundary"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3039", "topic": "successorFoldStates copies every catalog-admitted plan row", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B049-009"], "canonical_claim": "`successorFoldStates` copies every catalog-admitted plan row into the mutable successor seal so later coverage and cleanup updates do not mutate the plan and every fold target has a successor row.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": [".superpowers/sdd/2026-07-28-cas-ref-chain-stage-b-catalog/task-5-checkpoint75b-round-plan-report.md#implemented-boundary"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3040", "topic": "RefTableState::Removed and its exact contract", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B050-001", "B050-006", "B050-012", "B050-016", "B050-029"], "canonical_claim": "`RefTableState::Removed` and its exact `remove_txn_id` remain in-memory terminal state, including terminal log replay, removal ordering, mutation refusal, stale-handle behavior, and fold-cleanup evidence. `stateFromSnapshot` must explicitly initialize the returned in-memory state as `Live` because the default `RefTableState` is `Removed`. After Slice C, `RefTableState` still defaults to `RefLifecycle::Removed`, retains optional `remove_txn_id`, and terminal replay assigns the exact transaction id. `stateFromSnapshot` assigns `RefLifecycle::Live` explicitly rather than relying on the opposite-valued `RefTableState` default. In-memory `remove_txn_id`, `RefCleanupEvidence{remove_txn_id}`, and `RefLifecycle::Removed` remain intentional: they identify a terminal runtime, provide fold proof for pre-fold catalog drain, and distinguish terminal replay from an empty runtime.", "issue_ids": ["T5"], "sources": [".superpowers/sdd/task-5-step9-c-brief.md#L12-L12", ".superpowers/sdd/task-5-step9-c-brief.md#L7-L7", ".superpowers/sdd/task-5-step9-c-report.md#L16-L18", ".superpowers/sdd/task-5-step9-c-report.md#L27-L27", ".superpowers/sdd/task-5-step9-d-report.md#L47-L55"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3041", "topic": "S43 performs its no-absorption verdict before", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B050-025"], "canonical_claim": "S43 performs its no-absorption verdict before a recognizable life-2 insert; only that insert forces lazy namespace admission, and the inserted row must be the only visible content.", "issue_ids": ["S43"], "sources": [".superpowers/sdd/task-5-step9-d-report.md#L18-L21"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3042", "topic": "Pool::dropNamespace consists of the Removing catalog", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B050-046"], "canonical_claim": "`Pool::dropNamespace` consists of the `Removing` catalog transition, folded terminal evidence, exact catalog-row removal, and independent perpetual-janitor reclamation.", "issue_ids": ["T5"], "sources": [".superpowers/sdd/2026-07-28-cas-ref-chain-stage-b-catalog/task5-step9-10a-report.md#L7-L10"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3043", "topic": "The namespace-file contract fixture removes life", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B050-051"], "canonical_claim": "The namespace-file contract fixture removes life 1 through `CasRefCatalog::deleteCompletedRemoving` with cleanup evidence and a held fence before admitting life 2 as `Live`.", "issue_ids": ["T6"], "sources": [".superpowers/sdd/2026-07-28-cas-ref-chain-stage-b-catalog/task6-ns-file-contract-report.md#L12-L15"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3044", "topic": "The decommission-to-heal flow is two-phase: the", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B050-094"], "canonical_claim": "The decommission-to-heal flow is two-phase: the first call refuses with rows in `Removing`, a GC round deletes the catalog-only rows, and the second call retires the slot.", "issue_ids": ["T3"], "sources": [".superpowers/sdd/2026-08-02-cas-stage-b-remaining/unattended-log.md#L412-L438"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3045", "topic": "DiskObjectStorage::supportsHardLinks returns true for CAS to", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B051-060"], "canonical_claim": "`DiskObjectStorage::supportsHardLinks` returns true for CAS to enable mutation, lightweight `DELETE`, and data-`ALTER` capability checks without selecting a per-file autocommit path.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/upstream-patch-inventory.md#disk-object-storage-cpp"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3046", "topic": "For ContentAddressed disks, checkAlterPartitionIsPossible allows DROP", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B051-094"], "canonical_claim": "For `ContentAddressed` disks, `checkAlterPartitionIsPossible` allows `DROP`, `ATTACH`, `REPLACE`, `MOVE`, `FETCH`, `FREEZE`, `UNFREEZE`, and `FORGET`, and rejects other partition commands fail-closed.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/upstream-patch-inventory.md#merge-tree-data-cpp"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3047", "topic": "MergeTreeDeduplicationLog::load treats a missing logs_dir as", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B051-096"], "canonical_claim": "`MergeTreeDeduplicationLog::load` treats a missing `logs_dir` as normal for both Plain and `ContentAddressed` storage and still creates `current_writer`, because neither storage materializes empty directories.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/upstream-patch-inventory.md#merge-tree-deduplication-log-cpp"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3048", "topic": "Per-part write-time files uuid.txt contract", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M-006", "AUDIT-M2-020"], "canonical_claim": "Per-part write-time files uuid.txt, txn_version.txt, and metadata_version.txt are ordinary PartManifest entries, not a separate mutable sidecar; a single-file change on an already-committed part resolves through a committed-ref repoint that carries every unchanged entry forward and republishes one manifest, and a byte-identical rewrite is a zero-pool-mutation no-op (repointRef). Since the all-tree part-files migration, filling in the creation CSN on COMMIT and locking/unlocking the removal TID on an already-committed part are ordinary committed-ref standalone writes through `repointRef`, not a special mutable-only case: every OTHER committed manifest entry is carried forward and a byte-identical rewrite resolves through `repointRef`'s byte-equal check with zero pool mutations.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/01-architecture.md#part-manifests", "docs/superpowers/cas/03-writer-protocol.md#L575-L596"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3049", "topic": "A SQL DROP PART/DROP PARTITION is", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M-017", "B035-073"], "canonical_claim": "A SQL DROP PART/DROP PARTITION is modeled as removal supersession: an additive commit creates a real part ref, a removal creates an empty covering tombstone ref promoted from MergeTreeData::dropPartImpl's local Outdated empty covering part into a persisted, Active, covering ref using the existing ActiveDataPartSet/getActiveContainingPart covering machinery. This persisted part ref is distinct from the D1 shard-object tombstone, which is a journal marker letting GC reclaim an empty shard object.; SQL DROP PART or DROP PARTITION is represented by a covering tombstone ref in the CAS model, distinct from the D1 shard-object tombstone.", "issue_ids": ["D1"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/01-architecture.md#drop-supersession", "docs/superpowers/cas/CONSOLIDATION-COVERAGE.md#blocking-gaps", "docs/superpowers/cas/CONSOLIDATION-COVERAGE.md#gap-fill"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3050", "topic": "DROP PART has a documented weak", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M-018"], "canonical_claim": "DROP PART has a documented weak guarantee under a mid-range race with a concurrent merge: the merge may produce a real covering part carrying the data, so DROP PART can end up merged into a bigger part instead of deleting it (ReplicatedMergeTreeLogEntryData::getVirtualPartNames returns {} for DROP_PART). The persisted tombstone for a mid-range single part is therefore conditional/reconciled, not unconditional: if a real covering part wins the race, the tombstone must not strand or double-cover.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/01-architecture.md#drop-supersession"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3051", "topic": "A transactional merge/mutation spanning multiple parts", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-022"], "canonical_claim": "A transactional merge/mutation spanning multiple parts is keyed by a per-part staging map, `parts: (namespace, ref) -> PartStaging`, rather than a single `(table_uuid, part_name)`; a deferred rename (`tmp_merge_X -> X`) moves the staging entry to the destination key and merges it with any destination entry a deferred-window `txn_version.txt` write already created. `commit` takes the `gc_lock` once and iterates `parts`, applying the whole-part publish or the mutable-only branch per entry.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/03-writer-protocol.md#L625-L654"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3052", "topic": "CAS refuses BACKUP via temporary hard", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-077"], "canonical_claim": "CAS refuses BACKUP via temporary hard links for Ordinary (non-UUID) databases with `SUPPORT_IS_DISABLED`, while Atomic databases back up normally; partition commands are refused outside an explicit allowlist (DROP_PARTITION, DROP_DETACHED_PARTITION, FORGET_PARTITION, ATTACH_PARTITION, REPLACE_PARTITION, MOVE_PARTITION, FETCH_PARTITION, FREEZE_*, UNFREEZE_*); `supportsChmod`/`supportsStat` are false and `getHardlinkCount` returns 0; bucket versioning must not be enabled and is probed and refused; and `<readonly>1</readonly>` disks take no mount claim, run no probe, no writes, and no GC.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/11-walkthrough.md#non-goals"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3053", "topic": "S06/S07 (10000/20000-column wide parts) confirmed the", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-143"], "canonical_claim": "S06/S07 (10000/20000-column wide parts) confirmed the manifest encode/decode works correctly at scale: a 10000-column wide part commits under the manifest hard cap with no LIMIT_EXCEEDED needed, and the 1M-entry manifest cap is unreachable at any SQL-feasible column count (three orders of magnitude below 20000).", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-03-scenarios-full-scale-campaign.md#s06v3"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3054", "topic": "The removal driver resumes entry deletion", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-341"], "canonical_claim": "The removal driver resumes entry deletion idempotently on the owning writer's next mount after a stop in the `Removing`-without-`_ckpt` window; it never re-creates `_ckpt`, and the dead-root branch belongs to the decommission path instead.", "issue_ids": ["M1"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-31-stage-b-strategic-review-fable-round3.md#missing"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3055", "topic": "namespaceIsRemoved gates its recreate-flip on the", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-343"], "canonical_claim": "`namespaceIsRemoved` gates its recreate-flip on the `_cleanup` marker and `dropNamespace` publishes a constant-size `Removed` snapshot; both reader-side predicates must be re-derived from catalog state (`Removing`, then entry-absent) before the `_cleanup` marker class can be deleted.", "issue_ids": ["M6"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-31-stage-b-strategic-review-fable-round3.md#missing"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3056", "topic": "The writer no longer synchronizes a", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B003-009"], "canonical_claim": "The writer no longer synchronizes a retired view or writer-side ack floor; it point-reads an add-only freshness meta at promote and copy-forward.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/07-history.md#4-transition-table-phase-11-deliverable"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3057", "topic": "The planned registry-free newborn protocol creates", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B004-089"], "canonical_claim": "The planned registry-free newborn protocol creates a precommit-state shard fenced to the current `gcRound`, and gates `promote` on the shard's own `fence_round` rather than a registry fence.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-01-cas-shard-incarnation-and-registry-removal.md#L220-L252"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3058", "topic": "A first publish into a new", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B004-106"], "canonical_claim": "A first publish into a new namespace uses the existing precommit machinery: the ref-shard becomes a precommit-state object, its `+1` is protected by the fold barrier, and there is no separate `pending-newborns` object.", "issue_ids": ["D1"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-01-cas-shard-incarnation-and-registry-removal-design.md#newborn"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3059", "topic": "Phase 2 makes CachedPartFolderAccess the owner", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B008-020"], "canonical_claim": "Phase 2 makes `CachedPartFolderAccess` the owner of freshness policy and terminal committed-ref primitives while `ContentAddressedMetadataStorage` retains path semantics and transactions retain staging orchestration.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-08-cas-part-folder-cache-phase2.md#phase-2-plan"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3060", "topic": "Write-through erases are cache hygiene rather", "target": "architecture/part-lifecycle", "member_ids": ["B008-045"], "canonical_claim": "Write-through erases are cache hygiene rather than a correctness dependency; `dropRefBestEffort` erases even when its underlying drop fails because durable ref state is then unknown.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-08-cas-part-folder-cache-phase4.md#task-2"]}
